WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:05.610
Before we discuss how to infer ancestral
states, I will pause to mention

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:09.890
that half a century ago, biologists
constructed trees from anatomical and

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.180
physiological properties
called "characters".

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:17.840
For example, here's a toy
character table for three species,

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.600
where the two characters considered are
whether the species have wings or not, and

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:22.910
how many legs the species have.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.520
So, this brings us to the problem of
trying to reconstruct a phylogeny

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.320
from a character table.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.480
Of course, I don't need Son
to swoop in and tell us that

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.430
this is currently not
a well defined problem.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:40.740
In the modern era, constructing
a phylogeny from anatomical characters

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.370
seems crazy in some circumstances.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.940
Just imagine trying to construct
a coronavirus phylogeny

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.250
from anatomical characters.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:55.320
If you think for a moment about how we
would construct an evolutionary tree from

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:01.760
characters, then one idea you might
come up with would be to cluster

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.620
all organisms having the same character
on one side of the tree together.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:10.330
For example, if we're considering
wings in stick insects,

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:14.630
we should probably put all winged stick
insects on one side of the tree, and

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:19.170
all of the wingless stick insects
on the other side of the tree.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.800
This strategy is completely reasonable.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.490
And, it's in keeping with
an over 100 year old postulate,

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.320
called "Dollo's principle
of irreversibility".

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.660
What this principle states is
that evolution is efficient:

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:35.470
it doesn't reinvent the same organ over
and over again such as insect wings.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:42.700
Yet, when we construct the stick insect
phylogeny, we see that wings were gained

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.990
or lost at seven different times in stick
insects alone, shown here by stars.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.910
When we then zoom out and
construct a phylogeny of all insects,

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:56.070
we see that wings have been gained or
lost hundreds of times.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.370
This may seem like a mystery, but

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.870
what's going on is that evolution isn't
reinventing the wings from scratch.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.140
The pathways needed for

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.690
flight aren't being eliminated
completely by eroding into pseudogenes.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:15.310
They're just being used for
something else in wingless species;

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.670
then, they're getting turned back on
when they're needed in a winged species

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:19.530
to accommodate flight.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:26.290
Accordingly, using genetic data
as characters has some merits.

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:29.140
We can bypass issues such as these.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.870
In fact,
if we're given a multiple alignment,

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:35.770
then we can just view this alignment
as a character table of its own,

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:38.610
in which
each row corresponds to a species,

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.490
and each column of the multiple
alignment corresponds to

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:45.630
an individual character with up to
either four or 20 different values,

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.988
depending on whether we're looking at
a multiple alignment of DNA (or RNA),

00:02:48.988 --> 00:02:49.599
or amino acids.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.112
So, the question then, is how to
take this character table of sorts,

00:02:55.112 --> 00:02:59.764
constructed from the multiple alignment,
in order to reconstruct ancestral states.

